{"name":"Feminin Nightshade","hex":"#4f4352","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"feminin","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Obsolete form of feminine."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/feminin"},{"term":"nightshade","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Any of the poisonous plants belonging to the genus Solanum, especially black nightshade or woody nightshade.","Any plant of the wider Solanaceae family, including the nightshades as well as tomato, potato, eggplant, and deadly nightshade."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/nightshade"}],"entries":[{"title":"Solanaceae","blocks":[{"text":"Solanaceae, commonly known as the nightshades, is a family of flowering plants in the order Solanales. The family contains approximately 2,700 species, several of which are used as agricultural crops, medicinal plants, and ornamental plants. Many members of the family have high alkaloid contents, making some highly toxic, but many—such as tomatoes, potatoes, eggplants and peppers—are commonly used in food around the world."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/8b/Brugmansia_lg.jpg/330px-Brugmansia_lg.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Solanaceae"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Illustration_Solanum_dulcamara0_clean.png/500px-Illustration_Solanum_dulcamara0_clean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Illustration_Solanum_dulcamara0_clean.png/1280px-Illustration_Solanum_dulcamara0_clean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e1/Illustration_Solanum_dulcamara0_clean.png/500px-Illustration_Solanum_dulcamara0_clean.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm